        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was Harry Harvey born?\n\nAnswer A: December 14, 1846\n  about: Harry Harvey\n  sense: birth\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Harry Harvey (December 14, 1846 – April 2, 1896), originally named Harry Huckman, was a member of the United States Army who fought for the Union during the American Civil War, where he was awarded the Medal of Honor.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: June 4, 1873\n  about: Harry Harvey\n  sense: birth\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Born: June 4, 1873, New York, N.Y.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Harry Harvey (Medal of Honor, 1865) Harry Harvey (December 14, 1846 – April 2, 1896), originally named Harry Huckman, was a member of the United States Army who fought for the Union during the American Civil War, where he was awarded the Medal of Honor. He was born on December 14, 1846 in England, but moved to Rochester, New York. He entered service in Rochester, New York, and became a Corporal of Company A of the 22nd New York Company. He was awarded the Medal of Honor for action on March 2, 1865 in Waynesboro, Virginia. There, he is cited\nDocument 2: Harry Harvey (Medal of Honor, 1901) Harry Harvey (June 4, 1873 – April 5, 1929) was a United States Marine who was awarded the United States highest honor, the Medal of Honor for \"meritorious conduct\" during the Philippine–American War. Harvey was born in New York City. He joined the Marine Corps from Brooklyn in February 1898, and was honorably discharged 5 years later. Harry Harvey is buried in the Los Angeles National Cemetery, California Section 60, Row E. Rank and organization: Sergeant, U.S. Marine Corps. Born: June 4, 1873, New York, N.Y. Accredited to: New Jersey. G.O. No.: 55, July\nDocument 3: Harry Harvey Sr. Harry William Harvey Sr. (January 10, 1901 – November 27, 1985) was an American actor of theatre, film, and television. He was the father of actor, script supervisor, and director Harry William Harvey Jr.. Born in Indian Territory, now Oklahoma, Harvey appeared in minstrel shows, in vaudeville, and on the Broadway stage but is best remembered as a character actor who appeared in more than three hundred films and episodes of television series. He co-starred in \"The Oregon Trail\" (1936), with John Wayne, \"Old Overland Trail\" (1953), \"Wyoming Renegades\" (1954), \"Ride Beyond Vengeance\" (1966) with Chuck Connors,\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
